1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,091
On the seventh of December, 1995, at four
minutes past ten at night, Universal Time,

2
00:00:06,091 --> 00:00:11,104
a small probe hit the top of Jupiter's
atmosphere at a hundred and seventy

3
00:00:11,104 --> 00:00:13,001
thousand kilometres an hour.

4
00:00:13,421 --> 00:00:17,657
Within a minute, the gas in front of it
was hotter than the surface of the Sun.

5
00:00:18,077 --> 00:00:21,265
It carried six instruments, a parachute
and a radio.

6
00:00:21,685 --> 00:00:23,476
It was never meant to come back.

7
00:00:23,896 --> 00:00:26,530
For fifty-eight minutes, it sent data
home.

8
00:00:26,950 --> 00:00:28,495
Then the signal stopped.

9
00:00:28,915 --> 00:00:31,575
This is the story of how far it got.

10
00:00:31,995 --> 00:00:37,655
And of what lies beneath it, all the way
to the centre, in a place no machine will

11
00:00:37,655 --> 00:00:38,414
ever reach.

12
00:00:40,114 --> 00:00:43,258
Jupiter is eleven times wider than Earth.

13
00:00:43,678 --> 00:00:46,441
More than a thousand Earths could fit
inside it.

14
00:00:46,861 --> 00:00:51,939
It holds more than twice the material of
every other planet, moon and asteroid in

15
00:00:51,939 --> 00:00:53,569
the solar system combined.

16
00:00:53,989 --> 00:00:55,967
And it has no surface.

17
00:00:56,387 --> 00:00:57,656
Nothing to land on.

18
00:00:58,076 --> 00:01:01,756
Just gas, getting thicker and hotter the
deeper you go.

19
00:01:02,176 --> 00:01:05,644
The probe's journey began five months
before it arrived.

20
00:01:06,064 --> 00:01:11,040
In July 1995, it separated from its
mothership, the Galileo orbiter, about

21
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:16,285
eighty million kilometres from Jupiter,
and coasted the rest of the way alone.

22
00:01:16,705 --> 00:01:19,608
Its first danger wasn't the planet itself.

23
00:01:20,028 --> 00:01:25,109
About fifty thousand kilometres above the
clouds, it flew through an intense belt of

24
00:01:25,109 --> 00:01:29,524
radiation, particles trapped and
accelerated by Jupiter's magnetic field.

25
00:01:29,944 --> 00:01:31,016
Hold on to that.

26
00:01:31,436 --> 00:01:35,290
We'll find out where that field comes from
at the bottom of this journey.

27
00:01:36,990 --> 00:01:41,776
Jupiter's gravity is so strong that
anything falling in from far away hits the

28
00:01:41,776 --> 00:01:44,906
atmosphere at close to sixty kilometres
per second.

29
00:01:45,326 --> 00:01:47,653
The probe arrived at about forty-seven.

30
00:01:48,073 --> 00:01:52,614
A minute after entry, it was slowing down
so hard that it felt two hundred and

31
00:01:52,614 --> 00:01:54,943
twenty-eight times the force of gravity.

32
00:01:55,363 --> 00:01:59,739
If you'd been on board, your body would
have weighed more than fifteen tonnes.

33
00:02:00,159 --> 00:02:04,477
Its heat shield was taking the hit from
gas at sixteen thousand degrees.

34
00:02:04,897 --> 00:02:09,701
Then, as it slowed, a parachute opened,
and it began to sink, measuring everything

35
00:02:09,701 --> 00:02:10,287
around it.

36
00:02:11,987 --> 00:02:16,662
At the very top of the atmosphere, the
pressure is only a tenth of what you feel

37
00:02:16,662 --> 00:02:20,927
at sea level, and the temperature is minus
one hundred and sixty degrees.

38
00:02:21,347 --> 00:02:26,700
A little lower, at the same pressure as
the air around you right now, it's still

39
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:28,574
minus one hundred and eight.

40
00:02:28,994 --> 00:02:34,069
Jupiter's weather happens in three layers
of cloud, about seventy-one kilometres

41
00:02:34,069 --> 00:02:35,021
thick in total.

42
00:02:35,441 --> 00:02:41,037
The top layer is probably ammonia ice, the
bright white bands you see from space.

43
00:02:41,457 --> 00:02:44,744
Deeper down are clouds of water, and they
make lightning.

44
00:02:45,164 --> 00:02:49,898
Juno would later find something stranger
in those storms: lightning flashing high

45
00:02:49,898 --> 00:02:52,761
up, where it should be too cold for liquid
water.

46
00:02:53,181 --> 00:02:58,232
The likely explanation is hail made of
ammonia and water slush, nicknamed

47
00:02:58,232 --> 00:03:00,792
mushballs, falling out of the storms.

48
00:03:01,212 --> 00:03:05,002
The Galileo probe saw less of all this
than anyone expected.

49
00:03:05,422 --> 00:03:11,311
Less water, less lightning, and only half
the helium scientists had predicted near

50
00:03:11,311 --> 00:03:11,886
the top.

51
00:03:12,306 --> 00:03:15,274
Years later, Juno helped explain why.

52
00:03:15,694 --> 00:03:21,447
The probe had most likely fallen into an
unusually dry spot, a desert patch in

53
00:03:21,447 --> 00:03:22,479
Jupiter's sky.

54
00:03:24,179 --> 00:03:26,829
Fifty-eight minutes in, the science data
stopped.

55
00:03:27,249 --> 00:03:31,556
At sixty-one minutes, the transmitter
failed, about a hundred and eighty

56
00:03:31,556 --> 00:03:36,163
kilometres below the point where it
entered, at nearly twenty-three times the

57
00:03:36,163 --> 00:03:37,479
pressure at sea level.

58
00:03:37,899 --> 00:03:40,644
Whatever was left of it kept sinking into
the dark.

59
00:03:41,064 --> 00:03:43,857
From here on down, no machine has ever
gone.

60
00:03:45,557 --> 00:03:49,280
To follow it further, we need a different
kind of explorer.

61
00:03:49,700 --> 00:03:55,439
In 2016, NASA's Juno spacecraft began
looping over Jupiter's poles, measuring

62
00:03:55,439 --> 00:04:01,550
the planet from the outside: its gravity,
its magnetism, and microwaves glowing up

63
00:04:01,550 --> 00:04:03,562
from deep below the clouds.

64
00:04:03,982 --> 00:04:07,292
And it found that the weather doesn't stop
at the clouds.

65
00:04:07,712 --> 00:04:12,910
The famous stripes are jet streams, and
they reach about three thousand kilometres

66
00:04:12,910 --> 00:04:13,227
down.

67
00:04:13,647 --> 00:04:19,155
The Great Red Spot, a storm twice as wide
as Earth, has roots between three hundred

68
00:04:19,155 --> 00:04:22,008
and fifty and five hundred kilometres
deep.

69
00:04:22,428 --> 00:04:27,709
A storm wider than our planet, with roots
so deep that the probe's entire descent

70
00:04:27,709 --> 00:04:29,535
would fit inside them twice.

71
00:04:31,235 --> 00:04:34,098
Keep falling, and the pressure keeps
rising.

72
00:04:34,518 --> 00:04:39,770
Hydrogen, the gas that makes up almost
ninety percent of Jupiter, is squeezed so

73
00:04:39,770 --> 00:04:41,936
hard that it turns into a liquid.

74
00:04:42,356 --> 00:04:45,122
There is no clear surface to splash into.

75
00:04:45,542 --> 00:04:50,221
The gas just gets denser and hotter until
you are no longer falling through air.

76
00:04:50,641 --> 00:04:52,752
You are sinking through an ocean.

77
00:04:53,172 --> 00:04:57,429
And perhaps halfway to the centre,
something even stranger happens.

78
00:04:57,849 --> 00:05:02,181
The pressure becomes so great that
electrons are squeezed off the hydrogen

79
00:05:02,181 --> 00:05:02,532
atoms.

80
00:05:02,952 --> 00:05:06,112
The liquid starts to conduct electricity,
like a metal.

81
00:05:07,812 --> 00:05:11,917
This is the answer to the danger at the
start of our journey.

82
00:05:12,337 --> 00:05:17,641
An ocean of liquid metal, churning inside
a planet that spins once every ten hours,

83
00:05:17,641 --> 00:05:19,303
works like a giant dynamo.

84
00:05:19,723 --> 00:05:25,406
It generates a magnetic field sixteen to
fifty-four times as powerful as Earth's.

85
00:05:25,826 --> 00:05:30,212
That's the field that trapped the
radiation the probe flew through on its

86
00:05:30,212 --> 00:05:30,633
way in.

87
00:05:31,053 --> 00:05:33,193
Juno mapped it, and it's lumpy.

88
00:05:33,613 --> 00:05:38,788
Near the equator sits an intense patch
scientists call the Great Blue Spot, and

89
00:05:38,788 --> 00:05:43,045
it is slowly drifting, dragged around by
the planet's deep winds.

90
00:05:44,745 --> 00:05:46,293
And at the very centre?

91
00:05:46,713 --> 00:05:51,334
For decades, the diagrams showed a compact
ball of rock and ice.

92
00:05:51,754 --> 00:05:54,559
Juno's gravity measurements found
something else.

93
00:05:54,979 --> 00:05:58,516
The core looks much larger than expected,
and not solid.

94
00:05:58,936 --> 00:06:04,256
Partly dissolved, its heavy elements
smeared out into the metallic hydrogen

95
00:06:04,256 --> 00:06:04,965
around it.

96
00:06:05,385 --> 00:06:10,175
In some models, that fuzzy core stretches
more than halfway from the centre to the

97
00:06:10,175 --> 00:06:10,817
cloud tops.

98
00:06:11,237 --> 00:06:14,339
How does a planet end up with a core like
that?

99
00:06:14,759 --> 00:06:16,589
One idea is a collision.

100
00:06:17,009 --> 00:06:22,004
Early in Jupiter's history, a young planet
about ten times the mass of Earth may have

101
00:06:22,004 --> 00:06:26,528
hit it head-on, hard enough to shatter the
original core and stir it outward.

102
00:06:26,948 --> 00:06:28,261
It's a hypothesis.

103
00:06:28,681 --> 00:06:29,948
Nobody knows yet.

104
00:06:30,368 --> 00:06:35,177
But if it's right, the heart of the
biggest planet in the solar system still

105
00:06:35,177 --> 00:06:38,340
carries the scar of a crash billions of
years old.

106
00:06:40,040 --> 00:06:44,567
Back at the top of the clouds, those
fifty-eight minutes of data still matter.

107
00:06:44,987 --> 00:06:49,991
Everything below that point, we know only
from measurements taken from far outside.

108
00:06:50,411 --> 00:06:53,564
Jupiter isn't a ball of gas with a rock in
the middle.

109
00:06:53,984 --> 00:06:58,917
It's a planet with storms rooted hundreds
of kilometres deep, an ocean of liquid

110
00:06:58,917 --> 00:07:01,199
metal, and a core that never settled.

111
00:07:01,619 --> 00:07:05,965
And our first real look inside it came
from a little machine that fell in, and

112
00:07:05,965 --> 00:07:06,857
never came back.

113
00:07:07,277 --> 00:07:12,850
If you want more stories like this, told
with real images and real numbers,

114
00:07:12,850 --> 00:07:13,593
subscribe.

115
00:07:14,013 --> 00:07:16,380
Every source is in the description.
